.Dd July 8, 2026
.Dt RENICE 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm renice
.Nd alter priority of running processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Ar priority
.Oo Op Fl p
.Ar pid ... Oc
.Op Fl g Ar pgrp ...
.Op Fl u Ar user ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
alters the scheduling priority
.Pq nice value
of one or more running processes.
.Ar priority
is an absolute nice value in the range
.Fl 20
(most favourable scheduling) to
.Li 19
(least favourable); it may be preceded by
.Fl n
for compatibility.
Values outside this range are clamped to the nearest limit.
.Pp
The operands following the priority are process IDs by default.
The flags below change how subsequent operands are interpreted:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p
Interpret the following operands as process IDs (the default).
.It Fl g
Interpret the following operands as process-group IDs.
.It Fl u
Interpret the following operands as users, given either as a login
name or a numeric user ID.
.El
.Pp
Users other than the super-user may only alter the priority of
processes they own, and may only
.Em raise
the nice value (lower the priority); the kernel enforces this and
.Nm
reports the resulting error.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Change the priority of process IDs 987 and 32 to 5:
.Pp
.Dl renice 5 987 32
.Pp
Give all processes owned by
.Ar daemon
the least favourable priority:
.Pp
.Dl renice 19 -u daemon
.Pp
Favour process group 100 (super-user only):
.Pp
.Dl renice -10 -g 100
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nice 1 ,
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr setpriority 2
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility conforms to
.St -p1003.1 .
